A||||5||theogre||Service Notice: Rubber Brake Lines||||4||
Z||000000||theogre||05-02-2001||08:21 PM||||I've seen/heard a number of comments about the lining of the rubber brake lines swelling and cutting off fluid.<P>Something sounded odd about that... So like everything else I dug around to find out what would cause it.<P>In short only one thing will cause it. Contamination of the brake fluid. It's usually petroleum products but other things may contribute. (It is posible, but unlikely, that oil on the exterior of the hose could cause it. The exterior rubber is oil resistant. The exterior cover would have to be in very bad shape.)<P>(Many of you are already aware, but the oil will also ruin the seals in the cylinders...)<P>To prevent this....<P>Never use any petroleum based product on the caliper or the slaves in drum brakes. The oil can work around the boots and into the brake fluid.<P>If you have to use WD-40 and such to remove a stuck bolt or bleeder make sure you clean away the oil afterward with brake cleaner. Run plenty of fluid out the bleeder to rinse out oil.<P>When rebuilding, all oil must be removed. Use only clean brake fluid to lube seals.<P>If you have already had this problem, make sure all the fluid is replaced.<P>Also make sure that the oil hasn't damaged the balance/proportioning block next to the Master Cylinder! (Indiana Resto Guy can tell you about that one.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )<P>It takes very little oil to swell the seals and hoses. New/rebuilt calipers and Master Cylinders can be destroyed very quickly unless the contaminated fluid is removed. <P>Worse, the rear caliper piston may have internal parts that can be damaged. You can't fix them. the pistons have to be replaced. This sort of failure can bind the caliper and/or make the handbrake not work.<P>------------------<BR><I><B>Screamin Yellow Zonkers... If it's Screamin and Yellow, I ain't eatin it.</B></I>||||reg||4
Z||000001||KissMySSFiero||05-02-2001||08:43 PM||||im not sure if you talking about the same thing that im gonna say but it sounds similar.   <BR>some people clamp of brake lines when doing work on them to prevent from having to bleed them.  the shop i used to work at this was pretty much standard.  it wont damage the line if dont properly.  but if you use vise grips with a rough edge it can tear the inside of the brake line.  what happens if its torn is the inner liner acts as a valve.  when you hit the brakes hard it lets fluid in,  but not back out.  this locks the caliper.  after a while the caliper keeps getting more fluid and BAM.  it blows somewhere,  this happened to one of our techs and he obviously had to fix it.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierospeeder||05-02-2001||09:37 PM||||Always wondered about that tail.  Swelling of the lines.  <P>I watched shadetree mechanic and they would rebuild calipers and suggested rubbing brakefluid on the seals to lubricate it while putting the pistons in.  <P>I think the only time your suppose to use oil is on the injector orings and it has to be very light oil, and silicone will ruin the oxygen sensor.<P>Oil will eat through rubber and belts.  You have to lookout for stuff like that when your engine is leaking fluid.<P>thanks theogre||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||05-02-2001||11:16 PM||||Kiss,<P>Good point. I won't do that and I forgot many shops still do. Pinching the lines is just a bad idea.<P>When I'm trying to stop fluid from a disconnected line I fold a section of rubber or soft plastic over the banjo and pinch it with a spring clamp. (Depending on the clamp you might need a couple washers to spread the load for a good seal... I just use the old coppers. ) This will do no damage to the banjo or the line.<P>That or plug them with a section of dowel. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||SteveJ||05-03-2001||09:21 AM||||VICE GRIPS ON MY BRAKE HOSES???? !!!!! <P>No wonder I don't like to take my car in for shop repairs. Tell me KissMySSFiero would that shop also charge full price a couple months later to replace the brake hoses?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wolfhound||05-03-2001||09:44 AM||||What happens to stop the flow in the brake line is not from swelling. The brake line is three layers of different rubber compounds. the inter is soft and weak but forms a good seal the center is reinforced so your break line don't just inflate with the pressure and the outer is a weather protective layer.<BR> If you notice the problem almost always occurs on the front where steering causes the line to flex more. These compounds deteriorate with age and temperature changes. If the inner liner separates and breaks, it acts as an inline valve and will not let the caliper return to position.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
